      <RURComments xml:space="preserve">
                <windows>
You have previously upgraded all of the windows on the main floor of the house &amp; the 3 in the basement are still the older original aluminum slider windows.

If you decide to replace them, please choose ENERGY STAR® qualified windows, I recommend full frame replacement windows that are triple pane, double low-E, argon gas filled, hinged or fixed-frame window, with a U-Value (rate of heat loss) no higher than 1.22 W/m²K. For your house, I modeled a more efficient window with a U-Value no higher than 1.05 W/m²K.

Savings:
By replacing these 3 basement windows in your house with ones that meets the criteria listed above, you could potentially save $ 72 per year in energy costs and decrease your annual energy usage by 2.0 GJ. There are currently no rebates available for installing new windows from the GNWT Energy Efficiency Incentive Program.</windows>
                <VentilationSystem>
VENTILATION - HEAT RECOVERY VENTILATION (HRV)

Your house is currently very air tight and the software indicates it could use some additional ventilation. Proper ventilation protects a home from unwanted toxins, pollutants, and odors and can also help protect the overall structure of a home or building by eliminating excess moisture from the air. An energy-efficient Heat Recovery Ventilation  (HRV) unit is one of the best ways to control indoor air quality.

Your house currently has very little exhaust ventilation: 2 x bathroom fans and the clothes dryer .......... however these are not operated on a regular basis, so the air in the house can quickly become stale. A new ENERGY STAR® Heat Recovery Ventilation (HRV) unit may be what your house requires - one that has its' own circulation fans, control module and booster fan option for when you feel you could use a bit more "fresh air". We highly recommend that homeowners choose high efficiency HRV's (with a minimum 75% heat recovery efficiency) and low electricity use ECM circulation fans.

There are a few options that you could explore:

1. You could have a 6" Fresh Air Supply pipe brought in through the front header space of the basement and directly connected to the return air plenum of the furnace. These normally have an "in-line damper" that you can manually adjust from fully closed to fully open. You can operate your bathroom fans on a "timer switch" that will draw some stale air out, while the fresh air supply allows some back in. Also, each time your furnace operates during the winter, it will draw in a bit of fresh air through this pipe. If you find the indoor air is a bit stale with higher humidity, adjust the in-line damper to be more open. If you find the indoor air to be dry with high static electricity, then adjust the damper to be more closed. There are ALOT of houses in Fort Smith that manage indoor air quality with a fresh air makeup supply pipe.

2. If you find the indoor air could really use some help and you don't want to just exhaust 100% of the interior heat out through bathroom fans .... than I suggest you research having an ENERGY STAR® HRV, with a minimum 75% heat recovery efficiency rating, designed and installed by an individual who holds current certification from an organization, such as HRAI.

Savings: By installing an ENERGY STAR® rated HRV, with a minimum 75% efficiency, this may increase your energy costs by $ 100 per year and increase your annual energy usage by 2.2 GJ. There are currently no rebates available for installing an HRV from the GNWT Energy Efficiency Incentive Program.
</VentilationSystem>
                <HotWaterSystem>
Your house currently has a 137 litre electric hot water tank. As there are many tenants living in this house, hot water use will be quite high and this small tank has troubles keeping up (some tenants said they may get a cold shower).

I realize the simple solution is to install a larger electric hot water tank, however I'm also aware that electricity in Fort Smith has become alot more expensive. As you already have oil equipment in the house, perhaps you may have an interest in converting to a direct-vent, oil-fired semi-on-demand hot water heater with an energy factor of 0.70 or higher (eg: Toyotomi OM-122DW). This equipment is on display at the Thebacha Campus in Fort Smith and all the new oil tech trades are being trained on how to install &amp; set them up. At the Arctic Energy Alliance, we frequently see them installed in Communities that are not served by hydro electricity, however we are seeing them more in hydro communities as well, as homeowners look for alternatives based on the rising cost of electricity.

For you, having a direct-vent, semi-on-demand hot water system would help you avoid running out of hot water for the tenants !

Savings: By replacing your current electric hot water tank with a direct-vent, oil-fired semi-on-demand hot water heater with an energy factor of 0.70 or higher, could potentially save $ 458 per year in energy costs, increase your annual energy usage by 3.8 GJ, and qualify for up to $ 750 in rebates from the GNWT Energy Efficiency Incentive Program.
 
To qualify for a GNWT Energy Efficiency Incentive Program rebate, the rebate application and sales receipt must be received within three months of purchase date. For a full rebate, qualifying products must be purchased from participating NWT vendors.

For more information and rebate application forms, please visit our website: 
http://aea.nt.ca/programs/energy-efficiency-incentive-program

NOTE: The GNWT Energy Efficiency Incentive Program is regularly reviewed and the rebate amount and/or eligibility criteria can change. In addition, rebate funding is provided on a first-come, first-serve basis contingent upon available funding. Please check our website before purchasing to determine current rebate amounts, eligibility criteria and if rebate funding is still available.</HotWaterSystem>
                <HeatingSystem>
Your house is currently heated with a relatively new 87% efficient oil furnace, venting up a 5" chimney. In addition, there is a 5" open combustion air supply pipe in the mechanical room to supply combustion air to this furnace.

The Thebacha Campus trains the new oil tech trades on the Granby Conforto 95%+ ENERGY STAR® qualified high-efficiency condensing oil furnace, including a high efficiency ECM motor to circulate the heat. This type of furnace is direct-vent (vented out the side wall of the basement, using 2 x PVC pipes) and will no longer require the existing chimney or combustion air supply. For your "future planning", I'll model the estimated energy savings of upgrading to this furnace.

Savings:
By upgrading to an ENERGY STAR® qualified high-efficiency condensing oil furnace with a minimum efficiency of 95% AFUE (including a high efficiency ECM motor to circulate the heat), you could potentially save $ 1,100 per year in energy costs, decrease your annual energy usage by 30.6 GJ, and qualify for up to $ 1,500 in rebates from the GNWT Energy Efficiency Incentive Program. 

To qualify for a GNWT Energy Efficiency Incentive Program rebate, the rebate application and sales receipt must be received within three months of purchase date. For a full rebate, qualifying products must be purchased from participating NWT vendors. 

For more information and rebate application forms, please visit our website: 
http://aea.nt.ca/programs/energy-efficiency-incentive-program

NOTE: The GNWT Energy Efficiency Incentive Program is regularly reviewed and the rebate amount and/or eligibility criteria can change. In addition, rebate funding is provided on a first-come, first-serve basis contingent upon available funding. Please check our website before purchasing to determine current rebate amounts, eligibility criteria and if rebate funding is still available.

PELLET STOVE HEAT FOR THE BASEMENT

With your furnace thermostat on the upper floor, you may find it a bit cool in the basement and perhaps you may consider supplementing with a wood pellet stove &amp; working with your heating contractor to see if you could use your furnace fan to circulate the wood pellet stove throughout the house.

Savings:
For now, I have modeled the expected energy savings if you were to use a pellet stove to supplement up to 75% of the heating for your basement. The calculated net savings (reduced oil cost for the furnace, less cost of wood pellets for the pellet stove) is about $232 per year. A qualified wood pellet stove is eligible for 50% of purchase cost up to $ 2,000 in rebates from the GNWT Energy Efficiency Incentive Program.

Even though the estimated annual savings are not that large, you may still just want to install a pellet stove in the basement to provide supplemental heat to that area, making your time downstairs more enjoyable.
 
For more information and rebate application forms, please visit our website: 
http://aea.nt.ca/programs/energy-efficiency-incentive-program

NOTE: The GNWT Energy Efficiency Incentive Program is regularly reviewed and the rebate amount and/or eligibility criteria can change. In addition, rebate funding is provided on a first-come, first-serve basis contingent upon available funding. Please check our website before purchasing to determine current rebate amounts, eligibility criteria and if rebate funding is still available.</HeatingSystem>
                <AdditionalComments>
LED Lights

Savings: Light Emitting Diode (LED) lights save about 80% compared to regular incandescent lighting, and about 20% compared to flourescent lighting. Floursecent lighting also contains mercury which is a hazardous waste material that requires special disposal. And ..... LED lights work well in the cold and can last a long time.

Rebates: In Fort Smith, LED light bulbs and tubes are eligible for a rebate of 50% of the purchase price up to $ 200 (per fiscal year)  from t he GNWT Energy Efficiency Incentive Program. 

To qualify for a GNWT Energy Efficiency Incentive Program rebate, the rebate application and sales receipt must be received within three months of purchase date. For a full rebate, qualifying products must be purchased from participating NWT vendors. 

For more information and rebate application forms, please visit our website: 
http://aea.nt.ca/programs/energy-efficiency-incentive-program

NOTE: The GNWT Energy Efficiency Incentive Program is regularly reviewed and the rebate amount and/or eligibility criteria can change. In addition, rebate funding is provided on a first-come, first-serve basis contingent upon available funding. Please check our website before purchasing to determine current rebate amounts, eligibility criteria and if rebate funding is still available.

-----------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------

Depressurization Test

The result of the exhaust devices depressurization test, which consists of turning on all exhaust equipment (2 x bathroom fans &amp; the clothes dryer), indicated a pressure drop of 4.0 Pascals. As this number is lower than 5 Pascals, the use of this equipment is not currently expected to cause combustion products to be drawn into your home, if you have any chimney vented appliances in your home that are not direct-vent. Your current furnace is chimney vented, and can be susceptible to back-drafting of combustion. If you proceed to install a wood pellet stove in the basement, please be sure it has its' own dedicated outside combustion air supply pipe installed and it is directly connected to the back of the stove.

During my assessment of your home, I did see smoke and carbon monoxide (CO) detectors in your home. Please ensure both types of detectors are always installed &amp; operational in your home, and not beyond their "replace by" date.

-----------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------

Ventilation

Your home currently has the following ventilation:
        - Kitchen range hood - does not vent to the outside
        - 2 x bathroom exhaust fans
        - Clothes dryer

If you choose to replace or install any exhaust fans, please select ENERGY STAR® qualified units. For a new bathroom fans, you should consider installing timer switches (where you select 5, 10, 15, 20 minutes) so they will only remain on for an adequate time to remove bathroom moisture and/or odours without the risk of being left on all day and wasting heat.

-----------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------

Fuel Costs - Fort Smith

The following Nov. 1, 2022 values, before GST, have been used to calculate energy costs and savings in Fort Smith:
Electricity - $18 min, $0.25 per kWh
Oil  - $1.35 per litre 
Propane - $0.90 per litre delivered
Wood - $256.00 per cord delivered; $100 per cord self-harvested
Pellets - $6.78 per bag, $289.74 per tonne bulk delivered

-----------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------

Disclaimer
The foregoing report is provided as an assessment of the visible components only and shall not be taken as a guarantee or an acceptance of liability for future performance of any part of the building. This report is prepared with reasonable skill and care within the limitations of a visual inspection on the inspection date. This evaluation and report do not cover code compliance issues set by the authority having jurisdiction.

-----------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------

Your Energy Advisor

Please do not hesitate to contact me if you have any questions.

Sincerely,

Ken Baigent, Contracted Senior Energy Management Specialist
Arctic Energy Alliance
